WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.290
Paramount's big-budget Hollywood adaptation
of Masamune Shirow's legendary manga series

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.780
Ghost in the Shell has attracted attention
for a lot of reasons — and they're not all

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.780
good.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.300
The manga has been adapted into several animated
movies and TV shows since its 1989 debut,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.660
and they've all deviated from the source material
a bit.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.650
But the Scarlett Johansson-led, live-action
remake has earned the most attention.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.490
Mostly because someone with the name 'Johansson'
doesn't seem like quite the best fit for a

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.760
Japanese character.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.320
With talk of Hollywood whitewashing abound,
the verdict already seems to be in when it

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.460
comes to the leading lady.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.339
But what about the other members of the Section
9 special ops team?

00:00:33.339 --> 00:00:35.010
Here's how they should really look.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.010
The Major

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.659
Major Motoko Kusanagi is a cyborg whose only
organic parts are her brain and spine.

00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:44.499
And when she first appeared in Shirow's drawings,
she was a slightly immature gunslinger with

00:00:44.499 --> 00:00:46.440
bright pink hair and purple eyes.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.469
In the anime, she's given a pretty dour makeover,
with black hair and blue-grey eyes.

00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:55.569
And thanks to its critical success, the character
had become most recognizable as an emotionless

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:56.690
killing machine.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.329
This is the angle director Rupert Sanders
and his team of writers have taken with the

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:04.330
live-action film, and it suits Scarlett Johansson
well, since she's already portrayed similar

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.820
characters in multiple movies.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.750
But, really, we all know what the problem
is here.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.259
Johansson addressed the issue of whitewashing
in an interview with Marie Claire, explaining,

00:01:12.259 --> 00:01:15.770
"[I would] never presume to play another race
of a person.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.229
Diversity is important in Hollywood, and I
would never want to feel like I was playing

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:20.590
a character that was offensive."

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.579
So, while there's no shortage of Japanese
actresses with blockbuster experience who

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:28.480
could have taken the role, and Johansson isn't
terrible to look at, it's just not true to

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.000
the source material.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.119
Batou

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:35.409
Section 9's lead investigator Batou is the
franchise's most prominent male character,

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:36.530
and that's never changed.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.999
The same can't be said of his appearance,
however.

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:42.729
He's had both a shaved head and a ponytail
on occasion, though the short-tempered gearhead

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:44.509
is best known for his white crew cut.

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:48.620
Hollywood has opted to keep Batou short and
trim just as he was in the 1995 anime, and

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.439
they've also kept one of the character's most
defining features: Batou's cybernetic eyes.

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.139
These disc-like objects bulge comically when
Batou loses his cool in the manga, which actor

00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:01.869
Pilou Asbæk based his portrayal of the character
on, rather than the anime.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:05.979
The live-action version of Batou is a pretty
solid match for the manga version, while anime

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:11.009
Batou is more of a towering jarhead, a man
with a long face that gives nothing away.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:15.530
Asbæk pulls off the hairstyle and manages
not to look stupid with the cybernetic eyes,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.090
though for a perfect score, he probably needed
more time in the gym.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.120
Aramaki

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.980
Lieutenant Colonel Daisuke Aramaki is the
man pulling all the strings at Section 9,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.819
having created the specialist cyber-crime
unit himself.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:33.099
In Shirow's manga he is often referred to
as the "Old Ape" because, well, look at him.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:37.489
Bald on top with crazy white hair everywhere
else, he made a few strides up the evolutionary

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:41.090
ladder in the animated movies and TV shows
that followed, though his hair and goatee

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.090
remained.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.610
Takeshi Kitano, a legend of Japanese cinema,
takes on the character in the Paramount remake,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.620
adding some much-needed respectability to
the project.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.640
Despite being 70 years old, the Battle Royale
star still carries himself like a younger

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.120
man, and for all his tenacity, youth is not
something associated with Aramaki.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.329
Kitano can make any role his own, but with
a much rounder face, missing the signature

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:06.630
wispy beard, he doesn't much resemble the
Aramaki fans have come to know.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:07.630
Ishikawa

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.750
Chain-smoking tech wiz Ishikawa isn't the
most prominent member of Section 9, but he's

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.530
served with both the Major and Batou.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.430
The cyber warfare expert started out with
a black bushy beard and an unkempt mess of

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.939
hair in the first manga volume, and he remained
rough and ready in anime adaptations.

00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:25.482
As the oldest field operative on the team,
Ishikawa has always been characterized a being

00:03:25.482 --> 00:03:29.469
a little haggard and less powerful than his
allies, only joining a physical fight when

00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:31.019
it's absolutely necessary.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:35.490
Australian newcomer Lasarus Ratuere doesn't
exactly fit the bill at first glance, mostly

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.160
because he's only recently hit his thirties
and he looks to be in pretty decent shape.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.430
For most roles, this would be a good thing,
but it also puts him at odds with the traditional

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.709
scruffy image of Ishikawa, who looks every
bit his age and is fond of cigars and whiskey

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:49.390
over fitness.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:50.390
Togusa

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:54.560
As far as the manga is concerned, Section
9 operative Togusa is an emotional wreck who

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.250
struggles with self-esteem issues and insecurities
about his ability to contribute to the cause.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.319
This side of the character is less pronounced
in anime adaptations, however.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:06.970
There, Togusa comes across as far more accomplished
and confident — both things he needs so

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.260
he can pull off that mullet.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.549
While Togusa's hairstyle is the character's
most recognizable feature, he's known for

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:17.190
going retro from head to toe, with a fashion
sense that often gets him teased by his teammates.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.340
The first thing you notice about Chin Han's
Togusa is his severely toned down mullet.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.700
The star addressed the issue of his character's
iconic 'do in the run-up to Ghost in the Shell's

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:26.720
release, saying,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.820
"There's very different kinds of mullet[s].

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.760
How big is the party in the back?

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.130
Rest assured, fans, we're going to have the
mullet."

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.970
While the actor insisted the the "family man"
side of Togusa would be respected, he isn't

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:39.940
much of a visual match.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.940
Saito

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.890
As nothing but a supporting character in the
manga series, Saito didn't even get an appearance

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.250
in the 1995 anime.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:50.910
It wasn't until the Ghost in the Shell: Stand
Alone Complex TV series that his character

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.300
became something more, as Section 9's sharpshooter.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.660
Their version of Saito is a former mercenary
who lost his left eye to The Major in battle

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.570
before later joining her at S9 and having
it replaced with an enhanced one.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.790
Saito's most distinguishing feature is his
hawkeye, which links with satellites to allow

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.690
him unparalleled accuracy with his long-range
weapon.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:13.450
Paramount got it right when they decided to
stick with his left-side eyepatch in their

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.510
remake, despite the English language manga
showing otherwise.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:20.810
That wasn't their only good decision regarding
the character: Yutaka Izumihara's casting

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.500
ticks all the boxes, including the signature
Saito scowl, making him as a close to a real-life

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.570
Saito as you could hope for.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:28.570
Borma

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:33.490
Like Saito, Borma was a minor supporting character
in Shirow's work who never made it into the

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.160
first anime, but he returned for the second
season of the Stand Alone Complex anime series.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.360
Unlike Saito, he's taken a huge detour from
earlier depictions of the character for the

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.910
big screen adaptation.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.900
Borma's main distinguishing feature is his
big, bald head, and that's nowhere to be seen

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.570
in the live-action adaptation.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:54.030
The studio opted instead for Tawanda Manyimo,
and a completely different look altogether.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.910
Though he isn't a short guy at six feet tall,
Manyimo doesn't have the bulking Borma build,

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:04.420
and if you took away his red eyepieces, he'd
be totally unrecognizable as the character.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.420
Kuze

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.860
Hideo Kuze isn't the most famous antagonist
from the Ghost in the Shell universe, as more

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.470
of an activist than a straight-up villain,
walking the line between freedom fighter and

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:15.470
terrorist.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:19.231
Not a manga original, Kuze came into play
during the Stand Alone Complex series, though

00:06:19.231 --> 00:06:23.360
the Kuze played by Michael Pitt won't be solely
based on that character.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.920
Director Rupert Sanders told Collider,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.400
"Kuze borrows a few facets from different
characters in the series.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.900
[...] He's kind of our own creation."

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.550
Pitt's Kuze has a far more artificial look
than the animated Kuze, but despite that,

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.690
the shape of his face matches up, as does
the hairstyle.

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:38.690
Not bad...

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:39.690
Thanks for watching!

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.830
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.820
